From d4ec454b3739272982b6113ac8c51e26aa8b0454 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Cunningham <justin@bulletprooftiger.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:07:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] gfx12 layer split: the ten exact decode switches of the
 gfx1151 table on by default (2x R9700, IQ3_S, --layer-split 26: greedy 108.0
 -> 115.0 tok/s, output unchanged); one GPU unchanged

#1478 put only the prompt half of the gfx1151 table into the gfx12
defaults, because the decode half measured slower on one R9700. Split
over two R9700s the same switches measured faster: there every expert
is resident, so the CPU computes no misses.

arch_default_env / apply_arch_defaults take `layer_split` (default
false, so the SYCL caller and every other path are unchanged). On
gfx1200 / gfx1201 with it set, the table is the five prompt switches
followed by STRATA_Q8_PACKED, STRATA_Q6_PACKED, STRATA_MMVF_ROWS,
STRATA_ATTN_LANECELL, STRATA_EXPERT_V2, STRATA_TSUM, STRATA_LFUSE,
STRATA_GDN_SPLIT, STRATA_QFUSE and STRATA_PLE_BATCH: the gfx1151
table's decode half without STRATA_SH_STREAM (not measured on a gfx12
split). All ten are in the gfx1151 table, which holds only switches
with byte-identical output. No bit-changing switch is added.

generate.cpp passes layer_split = --layer-split given and two or more
GPUs visible (cudaGetDeviceCount). The device count decides because
`--layer-split auto` resolves later and clears itself on one GPU, so a
one-GPU box never gets the decode half. On a box with two or more GPUs
a split that later runs on one GPU (--split-device 0, or
--split-skip-if-fits when the model fits) keeps the switches; they are
exact, so only the speed can differ there.

Opt-outs: STRATA_GFX12_SPLIT_DECODE=0 drops only the decode half;
STRATA_GFX12_DEFAULTS=0 still turns the whole gfx12 table off; a switch
the user set is kept. The start-up line names the "gfx12 (RDNA4),
layer-split table" and both opt-outs.

Measured on 2x Radeon AI PRO R9700 32 GB (Ryzen 9 5900X, X570 PCIe 4.0
x8 / x8, ROCm 7.2.4), Qwen3.8-Flash-Next IQ3_S, --layer-split 26 (all
24,576 experts resident), spec 4 + MTP, 3 runs each; greedy is the
geometric mean of prose / code / edit / reasoning at temp 0, sampled
at temp 1.0, prompts are cold reads:
- 0.1.43 stock (mean of two sessions, one before and one after):
  greedy 108.0, sampled 100.5, 4K 1,370, 16K 1,553, 64K 2,452 tok/s
- this commit: greedy 115.0 (+6.5%), sampled 106.6 (+6.1%), 4K 1,450
  (+6%), 16K 1,788 (+15%), 64K 2,792 (+14%) tok/s
- greedy output byte-identical to stock on all four prompts, in all
  three runs
- STRATA_HIP_WMMA=1 on top (opt-in, it changes bits) reads prompts
  faster still: 4K 1,759, 16K 2,376, 64K 3,556 tok/s

Checks: arch_defaults_test (no GPU) OK: the one-GPU gfx12 table is
still exactly the five prompt switches; gfx12 + layer split gives 15
entries with the prompt half first, no bit-changing switch, no
STRATA_SH_STREAM and no STRATA_VERIFY_QDEDUP;
STRATA_GFX12_SPLIT_DECODE=0 gives 5, STRATA_GFX12_DEFAULTS=0 none;
gfx1151 is identical with and without the split; other architectures
stay empty; a user setting wins on the split table. On the GPUs the
two-GPU start-up line named all 15 switches; with HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0
and --layer-split 26 it named only the 5 prompt switches. Built for HIP
(ROCm 7.2.4, gfx1201, Linux, GCC); CUDA and SYCL not built (the new
parameter has a default, so their callers compile unchanged). Docs:
DETAILS.md, AMD_HIP.md (RDNA4), MULTI_GPU.md (Measured).
