From b52a14a0b6382963096a9dd4449475f28f82a8ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Cunningham <justin@bulletprooftiger.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:40:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] gfx12 split decode switches: decided from the resolved
 split (every GPU gfx12, Linux, no --split-skip-if-fits), not the visible GPU
 count; STRATA_Q8_PACKED left out (inert on the measured IQ3_S pack)

The previous commit turned the decode half on for `--layer-split` with
two or more GPUs visible, looked only at GPU 0's architecture, and ran
on every OS. So `--split-device 0` (both stages on one GPU), an `auto`
split cancelled by --split-skip-if-fits, and a gfx1201 + other-AMD split
got the switches, and Windows did too, none of which was measured.

- arch_defaults: new pure function gfx12_split_decode(gpu_archs,
  may_collapse, measured_os, why). Yes for two or more GPUs, every one
  gfx12 (gcnArchName of each, not GPU 0 only), a split that cannot fall
  back to one GPU, and a measured OS. STRATA_GFX12_SPLIT_DECODE=0 or
  STRATA_GFX12_DEFAULTS=0 make it no; STRATA_GFX12_SPLIT_DECODE=1 makes
  it yes on any gfx12 run (Windows, one GPU, --split-skip-if-fits), as
  setting the switches by hand would.
- generate.cpp: apply_arch_defaults moves from right after argument
  parsing to right after the split is resolved and every used GPU's
  architecture is checked (the gpu_arch_problem loop). multi_gpu is
  final there for --split-device 0 and for `auto` with one GPU visible.
  The archs passed are those of CUDA0, every later stage's device and
  any --expert-cache-remote helper. Nothing between the old place and
  the new one reads a switch: every reader of the 15 gfx12 and 18
  gfx1151 switches is a function-local static in prefill/, verify.cpp,
  native_dense.cpp, native_head.cpp, mtp.cpp or the kernels, first
  reached when CUDA0's dense weights load (STRATA_Q8_PACKED) or later.
  The gfx1151 table is applied at the new place too, unchanged.
- --split-skip-if-fits is decided after CUDA0's dense weights load, so
  after the switches are read. An `auto` split with it therefore keeps
  the prompt switches only (conservative; =1 overrides).
- Linux only: the switches are the same code on every OS, but the gain
  was measured on Linux only, and 0.1.43's other gfx12 default (16384
  prompt chunks) is Linux-only for the same reason. The caller passes
  measured_os = false on Windows.
- gfx1200 stays in: gfx_arch_is_gfx12 covers gfx1200 and gfx1201 and
  0.1.43's gfx12 table already treats them alike. gfx1200 was not run.
- When GPU 0 is gfx12, --layer-split is given and the decode switches
  stay off, one start-up line says why.
- STRATA_Q8_PACKED is out of the gfx12 split table (14 entries, not
  15). It packs only Q8_0 dense matrices of an eligible shape; the
  IQ3_S pack's one Q8_0 projection (blk.47.ffn_down_shexp, 640 inputs)
  is too small, and the measured runs log no "packed ... Q8_0
  matrices" line. So it did nothing in the measurement, and its effect
  on gfx12 is unknown. The measured numbers stand for the other nine.

Claims are now qualified to what was measured: 2x R9700 32 GB, Linux,
ROCm 7.2.4, IQ3_S, --layer-split 26, all 24,576 experts resident; the
generated output (answer text and token count) of four greedy prompts
was identical to stock 0.1.43 in all three runs, and so were twelve
sampled answers at fixed seeds. That is a check of generated output,
not of bitwise intermediate results. Report with per-run data, configs
and output hashes: bench/results/2026-10-12-community-2x-r9700-iq3_s-
0.1.43 on github.com/justincinmd/Strata, branch pr/bench-2x-r9700.
Code comments, DETAILS.md, AMD_HIP.md and MULTI_GPU.md say so.

Checks: arch_defaults_test (g++ -std=c++20, no GPU) OK. New: the
gfx12 split table is 14 entries without STRATA_Q8_PACKED;
gfx12_split_decode is yes for 2 and 4 gfx1201, gfx1200 + gfx1201 and a
gfx1201 with its feature suffix; no for no GPU or one, gfx1201 +
gfx1100 / gfx1151 / gfx1030 in any order, an unreadable name,
may_collapse, and measured_os = false; =0 and STRATA_GFX12_DEFAULTS=0
win over a qualifying split; =1 is yes on one GPU and on a mixed
split. Built for HIP (ROCm 7.2.4, gfx1201, Linux, GCC).
arch_defaults.cpp and its test also pass g++ -fsyntax-only -Wall
-Wextra. CUDA and SYCL were not built (no toolkit or compiler on this
machine). A CUDA build never calls the new code (it is inside
STRATA_USE_HIP); the SYCL port calls apply_arch_defaults with one
argument, which still compiles against the defaulted parameter. No GPU
run of this commit yet.
